<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Ming Code</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Da Ming Lü</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Ming Code (Da Ming Lü) was the legal code of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a>, issued under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Hongwu Emperor</a> and finalised in 1397. Building on Tang traditions, it set out laws and punishments and remained in force, with adaptations, as a model for the later Qing code.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Work on a new code began in 1364, when the future emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Zhu Yuanzhang</a> had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuchang">Wuchang</a> and begun to call himself the Prince of Wu. In 1367 he ordered his Left Grand Councilor, Li Shanchang, to compile a lenient and simple code; the result, of 285 articles closely based on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-code">Tang Code</a>, was issued at the end of that year with the Great Ming Commandment. A revision by the Minister of Justice, Liu Weiqian, in 1374 was the first to bear the name Great Ming Code and had 606 articles.</p><p>Further versions followed in 1376 and 1389, and no earlier Chinese code had been revised so often in so short a time. The final text of 460 articles was promulgated in 1397, and the emperor ordered that it never be changed. It was not, though later emperors added their own rulings, and in 1585, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanli-emperor">Wanli Emperor</a>, 382 regulations or precedents were appended.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The 460 articles stood in 30 sections and seven chapters. The first set out general principles and the Five Punishments: beating with the light stick, beating with the heavy stick, imprisonment, exile and death. The other six covered personnel, rituals, revenue, military affairs, penal affairs and public works, matching the Six Ministries of the Ming government. Some 260 articles dealt with the duties of officials, and deceiving the throne could be punished by strangulation or decapitation.</p><p>Article 410 forbade anything that ought not to be done according to reason, with no more than 80 strokes of the heavy stick, while the use of analogy was tightly limited and needed review by the Ministry of Justice and imperial approval. The Ten Abominations, such as plotting rebellion and lack of filial piety, barred offenders from amnesty and from the Eight Deliberations that eased the punishment of people of rank.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Wu Dahai translated the code into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> in the early 17th century, and the Qing kept it largely in force before replacing it with the Great Qing Legal Code. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-joseon">Taejo of Joseon</a> adapted its criminal provisions from the 1389 text, and it remained the main penal law throughout the Joseon dynasty. Scholars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> studied it for legal reform, and 17 of the 722 articles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-code">Lê Code</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> show its influence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Great Ming Code” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The state maintained detailed population and tax registers, and the law was codified in the <b>Great Ming Code</b> (Da Ming Lü).</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
